Strategic tackling of radionuclide threat and freshwater crisis is among priority concerns for the global community. Among various pollutants, selenium has been listed as a toxic and priority pollutant by the United States Environmental Protection Agency (US EPA).[1] Although an essential element for living species, it becomes severely toxic above a threshold concentration of $>400~{\upmu\up g}$ per day. The stringent guidelines by the World Health Organization (WHO) emphasizes the issue by restricting the concentration limit of selenium in drinking water as 50ppb (parts per billion).[2,3] Encompassing high mobility and high solubility in water, the oxoanionic species of selenium viz. $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ , $S e O_{3}^{2-}H S e O_{3}^{-}$ etc. pose a challenge for containment.[4] The widespread sources of selenium discharge include industrial production, mining, coal and nuclear power plants. In particular, the discharge from nuclear power plant contains a radioactive isotope of selenium $(^{79}{\mathsf{S e}})$ with an extensive half-life of $3.77\times10^{5}$ years, thus demanding immediate containment to avoid lethal problems such as gene mutation, respiratory and neurological defects etc.[5] Another noxious pollutant i.e Arsenic is a severe water contaminant and a "class A" human carcinogen. Arsenic poisoning leads to harmful diseases such as cancer, skin diseases, neurological disorders etc.[6] Global emphasis on arsenic poisoning can be understood from the strict regulation by WHO stating $<10$ ppb of arsenic as the safe permissible limit in drinking water.[3] The release of arsenic into the environment includes both natural as well as artificial sources such as mining, industrial waste etc.[7] Generally, in water medium arsenic is converted to its oxoanionic form under oxidative conditions, and in the pH range of 6.7 to 13.4, mono hydrogen arsenate $(\mathsf{H A s O}_{4}^{2-})$ is the dominant form of As(V).[8]
Pertaining to this oxo-anionic chemistry, multiple pathways for sequestration of these oxo-anions have been explored via ionexchange, adsorption, etc. Diverse materials have been trialled aiming toward the capture of oxo-anions of selenium and arsenic including metal-oxides, magnetite nanoparticles, activated carbon, zeolites, etc.[9] but lower surface area, weaker binding, leaching, poor selectivity or production of secondary pollutant species are the limiting factors for efficient and selective capture in the aforementioned materials. Also, conventional approaches are ineffective for sequestration of certain oxoanions of such toxic elements. For instance, metal-oxides have unfavourable complexation  behaviour towards $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ necessitating exploration of alternate materials with robust capture mechanism.[10] Thus, the topical research efforts are devoted for the realization of scalable host materials to entrap oxo-anion pollutants which exhibit high capture performance, fast kinetics, selectivity and facile regeneration.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have evolved as a class of multifunctional materials posing concurrent precision and flexibility to engineer desired systems exhibiting plethora of applications including environmental remediation.[11] Cationic MOFs, a subclass of ionic-MOFs (iMOFs) have been realised as exceptional host systems for capture of hazardous toxic oxoanions.[12] Cationic MOFs render various levels of tunability in designing host systems with high selectivity via mimicking molecular geometry, hydrophobic character of toxic oxo-anions etc.[13] Additionally, the inherent ionic nature of cationic MOFs presents an elegant route to entrap toxic oxo-anionic species via ion-exchange pathway to tackle leaching issue.[14] Though having a robust capture mechanism, cationic MOFs have not been explored hitherto for sequestrating toxic oxoanions of selenium and arsenic, however few neutral MOFs have been investigated for the same.[10b,11f,11i,11k,11l,11o] In the present study, we sought to exploit for the very first time cationic MOFs toward sequestering oxo-anions of selenium and arsenic via ionexchange approach. An important aspect of ion-exchange phenomenon viz. shape memory effect of template anion has been utilized to provide selectivity, which can be realised efficiently in cationic MOFs wherein facile tuning of template is feasible via synthetic pathway.[13k] Additionally, with the trapped oxo-anions being an integral part of framework as charge balancing ions, potential leaching is ruled out. Cationic MOFs with the aforementioned attributes present an effective alternative in capturing oxo-anions in a desired selective manner. The preliminary criteria of water stability of cationic MOFs have hindered exploration of different applications in an aqueous medium.[15] Based on this background, we screened these parameters while exploring cationic MOFs targeted toward capturing oxo-anions of selenium $(\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-})$ and arsenic $({\mathsf{H A s O}}_{4}^{2-})$ from aqueous medium to extend the scope of porous crystalline solids. For an efficient ion-exchanger, the similarity of charge, shape and size of the anion in the host framework with the target anions viz. $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ and ${\mathsf{H A s O}}_{4}^{2-}$ would seemingly favour the process. Thus we narrowed our interest on cationic MOFs having sulphate $(\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{2-})$ as the exchangeable anion, as it is divalent, the geometry around the central atom is tetrahedral and has similar shape and size as of target anions. Further, we narrowed our interest specifically towards Ni(II) based cationic MOFs as ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ based MOFs offer enhanced stability in water medium. $[119,11mathsf{n},13\mathsf{h},13\mathsf{i}]$ Combining all the desired features, we selected a water-stable cationic MOF which meets all the abovementioned requirements viz. $\{[\mathsf{N i}_{2}(\mathsf{L})_{3}(\mathsf{S O}_{4})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}]\cdot(\mathsf{S O}_{4})\cdot\mathsf{x G}$ (hereafter referred as iMOF-1C), where $\mathsf{L}=$ tris(4-(1H-imidazol-1- yl)phenyl)amine, $\mathsf{G}=$ guest solvent molecules and $\complement=$ Cationic , previously reported by our group (Scheme S1a).[13g] Compound iMOF-1C is a 3D MOF having ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ metal nodes extended via a tridentate neutral linker having imidazole as terminal donor moiety. The asymmetric unit constitutes two sulphate anions; one of which is coordinated to the Ni(II) node while the other remains free in the void space (Scheme S1b).
The superior feature of the framework includes pre-aligned and readily exchangeable uncoordinated ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}^{2-}$ anions within the porous channels, having weak non-covalent interactions with framework backbone (Figure 1a). Herein, we demonstrate capture of toxic oxoanions of $\mathsf{S e}(\mathsf{V I})$ and ${\mathsf{A s}}(\mathsf{V})$ via utilization of cationic MOF by judicious selection of iMOF-1C as a selective sorbent posing high capacity and fast kinetics along with a rare crystallographic evidence for the ion-exchange process.
The bulk scale synthesis (gram scale) of iMOF-1C was realised via slight modification to reported procedure.[13g] This synthetic advancement is relevant towards targeting large scale applications; especially owing to the lack of such synthetic approaches in the domain of cationic MOFs. The bulk phase crystallinity and purity was corroborated via powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD)  patterns (Figure S1a). Initially, to test the stability and crystallinity of the compound, crystals of iMOF-1C were dipped in an aqueous solution of selenate $(\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-})$ and mono hydrogen arsenate $(H A s O_{4}^{2-})$ . Analysis under optical microscope revealed no changes to the shape and texture of crystals (Figure S2). Further, energy dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray (EDX) mapping confirmed the presence of selenium and retention of morphology (Figure 1b). Enthused from these observations, detailed studies were performed for selenate anion wherein microcrystalline powder of iMOF-1C were soaked in 100 ppm solution of selenate anion and characterized the obtained compound using various techniques. PXRD patterns revealed a similar profile as simulated pattern for iMOF-1C confirming retention of framework integrity (Figure S1b). Field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) analysis confirmed retention of block shaped morphology (Figure S4a). EDX mapping analysis showed homogenous distribution of both selenium (Se) and sulfur $(\mathsf{S})$ content (Figure 1b, S4b-4c). Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) showed peaks corresponding to the $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ anion (Figure S6). Elemental analysis confirmed the ion-exchange mechanism as the Scontent decreased post ion-exchange corresponding to exchange of ${\sim}1$ sulphate anion (SI). These observations suggest primarily the exchange of sulphate anions from the porous voids. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) also validated this observation as we observed peaks for both sulphate and selenate at $\sim167.4\:\mathsf{e V}$ and $\sim59\tt e V$ respectively (Figure S3, S5). Further, iMOF-1C was soaked in 100ppm aqueous solution of selenate $(\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-})$ and the supernatant was analysed by performing inductively coupled plasma (ICP) measurements at various time intervals. The data analysis revealed significant uptake within 60 mins of contact time of iMOF-1C to the selenate stock solution (Figure S7, S10, 2b). The data indicates that the kinetics is rapid for $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ uptake and a pseudo-second order kinetic model fits well to the experimental data.
