Further, to find out the maximum adsorption capacity of iMOF1C, we exposed the compound to varied concentration of $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ solution ranging from 100 ppm to 1500 ppm (Figure 2a, S8). From this data, the adsorption capacity was estimated to be 100 mg of $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}{}^{2-}/\mathsf{g}$ of iMOF-1C, in accordance to the Langmuir adsorption model which matched well to the experimental value. (FigureS8). It is noteworthy to mention that this uptake value is among the highest in the domain of MOFs for selenate capture (Table S1). Additionally, for any sorbent, selectivity is a crucial aspect for practical implementation, thus we performed selectivity experiments by exposing MOF to binary mixture of solution of anions viz. chloride $(\mathsf{C l}^{-})$ , nitrate $(N O_{3}^{-})$ , bicarbonate $(H C O_{3}^{-})$ , sulfate $(\mathsf{S}\mathsf{O}_{4}^{2-})$ , carbonate $(\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-})$ and selenate $(\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-})$ , as they are commonly encountered in waste water streams and groundwater.[9e,16] The solid powder retrieved was investigated using FT-IR $(N O_{3}^{-}/{\mathsf{S e O}}_{4}^{2-})$ , $(\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}/\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-})$ and EDX analysis for $(\mathsf{C l}^{-}/\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-})$ . FTIR analysis exhibited no peaks for the competing $N O_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ anion (Figure S12) while EDX mapping profile also revealed homogenous distribution of selenium through the samples with the absence of peak for chloride (Figure S23).The supernatant solution was analyzed by ICP measurements and we observed similar uptake performance even in presence of competing ions which is a crucial criteria for assessing sorbent for practical considerations (Figure S11). This highlights the efficacy of ion-exchange approach for trapping oxo-anions of weakly adsorbing elements. As a step towards seeking practical applicability, we exposed compound iMOF-1C to low concentration (20 ppm) and studied the uptake behaviour. We found that the compound had fast kinetics and exhibited significant uptake within an hour of exposure (Figure S9). These observations indicated that iMOF1C can serve affirmatively towards selenium oxoanion mitigation, and demonstrated the judicious choice of the MOF for affording selective trapping. Apart from selectivity, recyclability of a material is quite important for the practical consideration of a sorbent. Compound iMOF-1C was soaked alternately in 100 ppm solution of selenate salt for uptake and in 1000 ppm solution of sulphate salt for release. The supernatant was analyzed via ICP measurements for quantifying the uptake amounts while the sorbent material was characterized via PXRD and EDX mapping. ICP experiments revealed similar uptake amount for three consecutive uptake and release cycles (Figure S22). EDX mapping confirmed the absence of selenium in the regenerated phase (Figure S30). PXRD patterns also confirmed that the framework retained during multiple cycle of adsorptiondesorption of anion (Figure S28). Thus, this compound can be utilized towards effective mitigation of hazardous and/or radioactive isotopes of selenium with long half life which have similar chemical and physical properties. Similarly, capture performance was investigated for the extremely toxic oxoanions of arsenic i.e. ${\mathsf{H A s O}}_{4}^{2-}$ . For preliminary investigation, we soaked crystals of iMOF-1C in 100 ppm solution of mono hydrogen arsenate and characterized using viz. PXRD, SEM, EDX, IR, ICP, XPS analysis. The PXRD pattern matched with the pristine MOF sugesting retention of framework integrity (Figure S1b). FESEM analysis revealed block shape morphology while EDX mapping revealed a homogenous distribution of arsenic throughout the crystals along with the presence of sulfur (Figure 1c). The presence of sulfur is indicative of exchange of only free sulfate anions present within the voids. Further, the presence of the mono hydrogen arsenate was confirmed via FTIR with peaks at $823~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (Figure S6). XPS spectra displayed peaks at $\mathord{\sim}45\ \mathfrak{e V}$ corresponding to 3d orbitals of arsenic (Figure S15). Enthused from this, the preliminary sorption experiment was performed to assess the uptake kinetics by exposing 2 mg of MOF to 100 ppm solution of mono hydrogen arsenate and the supernatant was analyzed by ICP measurements. The analysis revealed a steep uptake profile indicative of fast exchange behaviour (Figure 2c) which fitted well with pseudo-second order kinetics model (Figure S16-19). To quantify maximum uptake amount,  concentration dependent experiment was performed. Microcrystalline powder of iMOF-1C was exposed to varying concentrations (100 ppm-1500 ppm) and the supernatant was evaluated by ICP measurements (Figure S17). The maximum uptake was calculated as $85~\mathsf{m g}$ of ${\mathsf{H A s O}}_{4}{}^{2-}/{\mathsf{g}}$ of iMOF-1C and the experimental data fitted well to Langmuir model (Figure S17). Further, selectivity was checked for the uptake of the ${\mathsf{H A s O}}_{4}^{2-}$ in presence of interfering ions such as chloride $({\mathsf{C l}}^{-})$ , nitrate $(\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{^{-}})$ , bicarbonate $(H C O_{3}^{-})$ , sulfate $(\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{2-})$ , carbonate $(\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-})$ in equimolar ratio. After exposing, the solid residue was characterized using FTIR (for $N O_{3}^{-}/{5}{\tt e O}_{4}^{2-})$ , $(\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}/\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-})$ and EDX analysis (for ${\mathsf{C l}}^{-}/{\mathsf{S e O}}_{4}^{2-},$ . FT-IR measurements revealed the presence of peaks corresponding to the oxoanion ${\mathsf{H A s O}}_{4}^{2-}$ and no peaks due to competing anions $(N O_{3}^{-})$ (Figure S21) while EDX analysis revealed the homogeneous distribution of arsenic throughout the compound (Figure S23) with no peak for $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ . Further, supernatant solution was analysed using ICP measurements and we observed appreciable uptake performance in presence of other common anions (Figure S20). We performed regeneration test for three uptake-release cycles by soaking 2 mg of iMOF-1C in mono hydrogen arsenate solution and eluted with sodium sulfate solution (1000 ppm). The supernatant was characterized by ICP while the solids were evaluated via PXRD and EDX mapping. ICP measurements revealed similar uptake amounts (Figure S22). Further, post release characterization via PXRD analysis showed retention of framework integrity (Figure S28). No arsenic was detected from the EDX profile (Figure S30). Like the previous case, we recorded the sorption behaviour at low concentration (20 ppm) and found a similar trend (Figure S18). These results suggests the ability of the approach to work under real-time conditions.
