Cationic MOFs seldom render structure-property correlation post ion-exchange, a quintessential feature sought after in material chemistry aiding fundamental understanding to the design principles.[12a,13h,13j]  Single crystal analysis post ion-exchange is limited to a handful of reports due to rupturing of crystals during ion-exchange process.[12b,12d,13c,13f,13i] To study the encapsulation of oxo-anions crystallographically, we exposed crystals of the parent compound to an aqueous solution of $H A s O_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ (Figure S2). Unit cell refinement exhibited similar unit cell parameters as the parent framework (Table S3). Structural refinements revealed presence of mono hydrogen arsenate anion along the 1D channel of framework with a subtle shift in positioning of mono hydrogen arsenate oxo-anion with respect to sulfate anion which could be attributed to the larger size of anion thereby offering insight into variable modes of host-analyte interaction in a cationic framework (Figure 3, S27). The bond lengths of As-O bond in mono hydrogen arsenate anion were found to be within the range 1.65-1.75 Å, comparable to As(V) based anion.[17] In addition, detailed analysis revealed a different interacting site of the mono hydrogen arsenate anion with water molecule present at the Ni(II) metal node via hydrogen bonding interaction at a distance of 2.72 Å which may play a key role in overall stabilization of guest anion (Figure S25-26). Further, we were also able to successfully obtain single crystal structure after selenate oxo-anion exchange. Even though selenate anions were found to be highly disordered in nature, but the positional occupancy within the voids was comparable to initial free sulfate anion (Figure S13).
In conclusion, we have successfully demonstrated bulk scale synthesis of a water stable cationic MOF viz. iMOF-1C with simultaneous capture of toxic anions of $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ and ${\mathsf{H A s O}}_{4}^{2-}$ .The framework exhibits fast kinetics and high sorption capacity i.e $100\ m{\mathfrak{g}}/{\mathfrak{g}}$ and $84~\mathsf{m g/g}$ for $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ and ${\mathsf{H A s O}}_{4}^{2-}$ respectively, which are among the highest values reported in the domain of MOFs. In addition, selective capture towards $\mathsf{S e O}_{4}^{2-}$ and ${\mathsf{H A s O}}_{4}^{2-}$ anions was also exhibited in presence of concurrent anions such as $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ and ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ which is crucial for practical applications. The regeneration of the framework viz. iMOF-1C was facile and multiple release-uptake cycles exhibited similar uptake amounts. Additionally, single crystal analysis provided a rare molecular insight between toxic anions and framework which could be correlated to nature and geometry of the guest anion. The present study opens new avenues for ionic MOFs to study the sequestration of oxoanions of less explored elements viz. selenium and arsenic, which are a potent hazard, and the insights gained from molecular level analysis could propel development of MOFs towards remediation of priority environmental pollutants.
